import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Canonical surface as the arrow calculus — design detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Canonical surface as the arrow calculus — design detail","type":"design","date":"2026-09-14T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","refs":["ADR-0100"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/0100-canonical-term-arrows.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/0100-canonical-term-arrows.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/design/0100-canonical-term-arrows.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="canonical-surface-as-the-arrow-calculus-—-design-detail" tabindex="-1">Canonical surface as the arrow calculus — design detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#canonical-surface-as-the-arrow-calculus-—-design-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “Canonical surface as the arrow calculus — design detail”">​</a></h1><p>Moved verbatim from ADR-0100 (ADR-0161 size budget). The ADR carries the rules; this page carries the context, tables, phases and trade-offs.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The canonical surface syntax (<code>~&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>***</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>do</code>, <code>=</code>) is intended to be arrow-native, but the current runtime keeps a mixed AST with <code>block</code>, <code>assign</code>, <code>function</code>, <code>call</code>, and <code>object</code> nodes. Those are not arrow calculus forms; they are lambda-style concepts that the design intended to eliminate.</p><p>The reference model is the <strong>arrow calculus</strong> used in the research prototype (<code>research/arrow-calculus-kernel-poc/PROTOTYPE.md</code>) and the papers of Hughes, Lindley–Wadler–Yallop, and Sanada:</p><ul><li><code>arr</code> — lift a pure function to an arrow</li><li><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> / <code>comp</code> — sequential composition</li><li><code>first</code> — act on the first component of a pair</li><li><code>choice</code> — branch on <code>Either</code></li><li><code>loop</code> — feedback</li></ul><p>Crucially, the research notes and the prototype explicitly state that the arrow calculus <strong>has no <code>λ</code> and no binder</strong> (<code>docs/explanations/local-data-without-closures.md</code>): &quot;The arrow calculus has no <code>λ</code>, so it has no binder, so it has nothing captured.&quot; Variables and <code>let</code> may appear in the <em>surface</em> and in an intermediate <code>Expr</code> (as in the prototype&#39;s <code>Var</code>, <code>Lambda</code>, <code>Let</code>), but the kernel <code>Term</code> does not contain them.</p><p>The internal <code>Term</code> kernel in <code>plugin-engine/src/term.rs</code> already matches this model: <code>Arr</code>, <code>Op</code>, <code>Seq</code>, <code>First</code>, <code>Choice</code>, <code>Loop</code>, <code>App</code>, <code>Handle</code>, etc. It does not have a <code>Lambda</code> or <code>Var</code> constructor. The <code>Arr</code> constructor is &quot;Sanada&#39;s <code>arr</code>&quot; — a pure constant arrow — and <code>Op</code> is the named operation form. <code>~&gt;</code> and <code>do</code> are therefore <em>syntactic sugar</em> that the parser/lowerer eliminates into point-free arrow graphs.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Make the canonical internal form the arrow-calculus <code>Term</code> with no <code>function</code>, <code>call</code>, <code>block</code>, <code>assign</code>, or <code>object</code> nodes. Every canonical program lowers to a <code>Term</code> graph built from the existing constructors:</p><ul><li><code>Arr</code> — pure constant arrow / lifted pure operation (<code>arr</code>)</li><li><code>Const</code> — pure constant value (used as an argument)</li><li><code>Op</code> — named native/foreign operation</li><li><code>Seq</code> — composition (<code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>)</li><li><code>First</code> — act on first component</li><li><code>Second</code> — act on second component (derived from <code>first</code> + <code>arr swap</code>)</li><li><code>Choice</code> — branch on <code>Either</code></li><li><code>Loop</code> — feedback</li><li><code>App</code> — higher-order arrow application</li><li><code>Handle</code> — capability handler</li></ul><p>Canonical lowering:</p><ul><li><code>x ~&gt; body</code> → point-free <code>Term</code> sugar. The parser/lowerer eliminates the variable <code>x</code> using <code>arr</code> of pure functions and the product combinators (<code>first</code>, <code>second</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>***</code>) as in the research prototype&#39;s &quot;Lambda-to-Arrow Translation&quot;. If <code>body</code> is not point-free expressible, it is lifted to a pure <code>FunctionValue</code> and wrapped in <code>Arr</code>.</li><li><code>f(x)</code> / <code>f y</code> → <code>App { callee: f, args: [x] }</code> when <code>f</code> is dynamic, or <code>Seq</code> when <code>f</code> is a known arrow.</li><li><code>do { a = b; c }</code> → <code>Seq(b, ...)</code> with the result threaded through the input. <code>=</code> is not a semantic node.</li><li><code>{ first: a, last: b }</code> → an <code>Op</code>/<code>Arr</code> that constructs the record from the arrow inputs.</li><li><code>a + b</code>, <code>a * b</code> → <code>Op { &quot;math.add&quot; / &quot;math.mul&quot;, ... }</code>.</li><li>literals (<code>10</code>, <code>7</code>) → <code>Const</code>.</li></ul><p>The existing generic AST/eval runtime is replaced or augmented by an engine that interprets <code>Term</code> directly. <code>eval.yaml</code> becomes a description of how each <code>Term</code> constructor is interpreted.</p><h2 id="rationale-for-arr-op-and-handle" tabindex="-1">Rationale for <code>Arr</code>, <code>Op</code>, and <code>Handle</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#rationale-for-arr-op-and-handle" aria-label="Permalink to “Rationale for Arr, Op, and Handle”">​</a></h2><p>The three leaf-like constructors are not interchangeable:</p><ul><li><code>Arr</code> = a pure, fixed arrow whose behaviour is supplied by the host runtime. The <code>~[FFI]~&gt;</code> grade on <code>call</code> in <code>handl-packages/ffi/ffi.hnl</code> is this pattern: a shallow, runtime-injected effect with no user-visible continuation.</li><li><code>Op</code> = an effect <strong>request</strong> with an explicit continuation <code>k</code>. It is required for user-space algebraic handlers because the handler must inspect and rebuild the continuation tree (<code>Pure v | Op op arg k</code> in the freer-arrow model).</li><li><code>Handle</code> = a handler scope / model that interprets <code>Op</code> requests and removes the corresponding grade from the row.</li></ul><p><code>Op</code> cannot be collapsed into a graded <code>Arr</code> of type <code>(HandlerEnv, A) -&gt; B</code>, because that makes the continuation an opaque function and the handler cannot pattern-match on the rest of the computation to rewire it. <code>Arr</code> + grade is the right primitive for host/FFI effects; <code>Op</code> + <code>Handle</code> is required for user-space algebraic handlers. The <code>Term</code> kernel therefore retains all three.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>Term</code> does <strong>not</strong> gain <code>Lam</code> or <code>Var</code>. The kernel stays combinator-based.</li><li><code>~&gt;</code> and <code>do</code> are surface sugar, not semantic forms.</li><li>A canonical-to-<code>Term</code> lowerer must implement the lambda-to-arrow translation, building pairs and fanout from <code>arr</code>, <code>comp</code>, and <code>first</code>.</li><li>All canonical tooling (parser, typechecker, formatter, linter) must produce or consume <code>Term</code> graphs.</li><li>Runtime eval engines and back-end compilers (JS, LLVM) interpret <code>Term</code> directly.</li><li>This is a breaking, multi-crate refactor.</li></ul>`,20)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};